// ============================================================================
// YOUR Firestore rules. This file is the SOURCE and it is yours — @omega.js/backend
// never rewrites the rules you write here.
//
// `omega build` COMPILES this file together with the framework half into
// dist/firestore.rules, and firebase.json points the emulator AND
// `firebase deploy` at that compiled artifact. Never edit the compiled file.
//
//   Framework half : node_modules/@omega.js/backend/templates/firestore.framework.rules
//   Compiled output: dist/firestore.rules  (GENERATED — do not edit)
//
// MERGE-BY-MATCH — how you TIGHTEN a framework rule. Firestore ORs `allow`
// across sibling match blocks, so a second `match /users/{uid}` could only ever
// widen access. Instead, a match block here whose PATH matches a framework
// block's (wildcard names do not have to agree) is MERGED into it: for an op
// both blocks declare, your condition is ANDed onto the framework's.
//
//   you write here          match /users/{userId} {
//                             allow create, update: if !isWritingAny(['xp']) && isEmailVerified();
//                           }
//   the framework ships     match /users/{uid} {
//                             allow create, update: if isUser(uid) && !isWritingFrameworkField();
//                           }
//   dist/firestore.rules    match /users/{uid} {
//                             allow create, update: if isUser(uid) && !isWritingFrameworkField() && (!isWritingAny(['xp']) && isEmailVerified());
//                           }
//
// Ops pair by NAME, so name the same ones the framework does (`allow write`
// beside its `allow create, update` would WIDEN, not tighten — the compiler
// says so out loud). An op ONLY you declare is appended as written (that op
// widens, as sibling blocks always did); a nested match of yours splices in; a
// path the framework does not define is yours alone and passes through
// untouched.
//
// Both halves land in the SAME `match /databases/{database}/documents` scope,
// so your rules may call ANY framework helper — declaration order does not
// matter. `is*` asks a question, `get*` hands back a value:
//
//   isAuthenticated()   isUser(id)          isOwner()
//   isAdmin()           isEmailVerified()
//   isWritingAny(list)  isWritingField(f)   isCreatingField(f) isUpdatingField(f)
//   getAuthUid()        getAuthEmail()      getRoles()
//   getExistingData()   getIncomingData()
//
// Fields are TOP-LEVEL: protecting `xp.total` means listing `'xp'`.
// See the framework half for the full list and what each one reads (`getRoles()`
// bills a document read per call, and so does every `isAdmin()`; the rest are
// free).
// ============================================================================
rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    // ─── Your rules ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // match /posts/{id} {
    //   allow read: if true;
    //   allow write: if isAdmin();
    // }
  }
}
